{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch1PdsSz8dJJwzbZUiQhK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YbqVbAQomhRenriFjfwbGz","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"report":"SCOPE OF PAYLOAD 25/32: one file, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh, two hunks: (1) memory/ is now copied as regular files only (find -type f -print0 loop) instead of `cp -pR memory/.`; (2) `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1` is set for the tar call. Context read: the whole candidate file, ceo-restore.sh:255-334, _restore_archive.py:40-189, CHANGELOG.md:274-298, and tests test_ceo_backup.py, test_ceo_restore.py:185-200 and test_restore_archive_appledouble.py:617-621. Everything was checked by reading; nothing was run.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED\n- Writes stay in place. `rel` is cut from find output under \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory/\". The prefix is quoted inside ${f#\"...\"}, so glob characters in AUDIT_DIR are literal. A trailing slash in AUDIT_DIR (find prints `x//memory/...`) still matches. find never puts `..` in a path. If the prefix were not stripped, the path would only nest deeper under the mktemp stage. Nothing can be written outside $STAGE_DIR.\n- Links: find runs in its default no-follow mode, so it does not go into linked subdirectories and leaves out linked files. No link from memory/ reaches the archive. The safety rationale in the comment (ceo-backup.sh:249-250) holds. The CHANGELOG line «archives only the regular files of memory/; a link there is left out» is honest. test_ceo_restore.py:192-198 covers a link inside memory/.\n- Fail direction: the loop body runs in the main shell (process substitution, not a pipe), so a failing `cp` still stops the script under `set -e` before tar and rotation run. That matches the old `cp -pR`.\n- COPYFILE_DISABLE=1: the comment is accurate. macOS bsdtar stops writing `._` AppleDouble members, and GNU tar ignores the variable. Per the CHANGELOG, restore refused every archive containing these members (`._.` included). A darwin-only round-trip test exists (test_restore_archive_appledouble.py:617).\n- Portability: `read -r -d ''`, ${var#\"pat\"} and `< <(...)` all work in bash 3.2, the macOS default. No Python in this payload, so the stdlib / Python 3.9 item does not apply.\n- Adopter blast radius: no hook, model, effort or governance surface is touched. No declared condition makes a claim about this file that the code contradicts.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n- None at P0 or P1.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. P2 · .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:262-265 (unchanged line, but now inconsistent with this delta and the new restore validator). `--include-plans` still uses `cp -pR \"$ADOPTER_ROOT/.claude/plans/.\" ...`, which keeps links as links on both GNU and BSD cp. `_restore_archive.py:112-113` refuses any member that is not a file or directory, anywhere in the archive, even without --restore-plans. Scenario: an adopter runs the documented cron with --include-plans and has one link under .claude/plans/. Every backup exits 0, but ceo-restore.sh refuses the whole archive, so the audit log and memory cannot be restored with the tool either. The data is still in the archive and manual `tar xzf` (DISASTER-RECOVERY.md:361) works, so nothing is lost. Minimal fix: copy plans/ with the same `find -type f -print0` loop used for memory/ (lines 251-255).\n2. P2 · .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:247-255. If \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\" is itself a link to a directory, `[ -d ]` is true but find does not follow it, so all of memory/ is silently missing from the archive. The old `cp -pR memory/.` followed the link. Under the documented cron (`> /dev/null 2>&1`) the run looks successful (exit 0) or prints «nothing to back up». This matches restore's refusal of a link target (ceo-restore.sh:258-260), but nothing warns. Minimal fix: `if [ -L \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\" ]; then echo \"warning: memory/ is a link; not backed up\" >&2; fi`, optionally plus a count of skipped links (`find ... ! -type f ! -type d`). No test covers this case.\n3. P2 · .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:255. find runs inside a process substitution, so its exit status is lost. With an unreadable subdirectory under memory/ (or any other walk error), the backup now leaves that subtree out and still exits 0. Rotation (lines 360-370) can then delete older complete backups in favour of partial ones. Before, `cp -pR` failed and `set -e` stopped the run with no archive. Minimal fix: run find into a temp list first and check its status, e.g. `find ... -print0 > \"$STAGE_DIR/.memlist\" || { echo \"fatal: memory walk failed\" >&2; exit 2; }`, then read from that file (and delete it before tar).\n4. P2 · .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:253 (same pattern in ceo-restore.sh:322, which is outside this payload). `dirname \"$rel\"` has no `--`. A memory subdirectory whose name starts with `-` (rel=`-dir/file`) makes GNU and BSD dirname fail with an invalid-option error. mkdir then creates only memory/, `cp` fails, and the whole backup stops with exit 1, a code the header reserves for usage errors. It fails closed, but it is a regression from `cp -pR`. Minimal fix: `mkdir -p \"$STAGE_DIR/memory/$(dirname -- \"$rel\")\"`, or use `${rel%/*}` with a top-level guard.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS vs THIS PAYLOAD: no condition is specific to ceo-backup.sh. Conditions 13 and 14 (scope and redaction) are consistent with what this payload shows. No inversion of the hook fail direction, no partial write over an adopter's file, and no dishonest changelog or comment line was found.","decision":"GO","sentence":"The one file in this payload, ceo-backup.sh, keeps every write inside its temporary staging folder, keeps links out of the memory/ part of the archive as its changelog and comments say, and stops macOS tar from adding the hidden members that restore refused; the four findings are P2s with no data lost, no hook or governance effect and no false declared condition."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3041,"cache_read_input_tokens":42921,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3041},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"693e57fe-d263-4dfa-87ac-840e80cc1fae","uuid":"eef50a9b-9662-49b0-a932-1da12b529113","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:21:44.494Z","request_id":"req_011Ch1PdsFpb2aQ93EcEizM5","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01YbqVbAQomhRenriFjfwbGz":{"report":"SCOPE OF PAYLOAD 25/32: one file, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh, two hunks: (1) memory/ is now copied as regular files only (find -type f -print0 loop) instead of `cp -pR memory/.`; (2) `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1` is set for the tar call. Context read: the whole candidate file, ceo-restore.sh:255-334, _restore_archive.py:40-189, CHANGELOG.md:274-298, and tests test_ceo_backup.py, test_ceo_restore.py:185-200 and test_restore_archive_appledouble.py:617-621. Everything was checked by reading; nothing was run.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED\n- Writes stay in place. `rel` is cut from find output under \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory/\". The prefix is quoted inside ${f#\"...\"}, so glob characters in AUDIT_DIR are literal. A trailing slash in AUDIT_DIR (find prints `x//memory/...`) still matches. find never puts `..` in a path. If the prefix were not stripped, the path would only nest deeper under the mktemp stage. Nothing can be written outside $STAGE_DIR.\n- Links: find runs in its default no-follow mode, so it does not go into linked subdirectories and leaves out linked files. No link from memory/ reaches the archive. The safety rationale in the comment (ceo-backup.sh:249-250) holds. The CHANGELOG line «archives only the regular files of memory/; a link there is left out» is honest. test_ceo_restore.py:192-198 covers a link inside memory/.\n- Fail direction: the loop body runs in th